---
title: "Inventory Management System"
category: "Commerce & Fintech"
difficulty: "Hard"
tags: ["inventory", "reservations", "consistency", "payments", "concurrency"]
---

## Overview

This system tracks limited stock across products (SKUs) and supports **time-bound reservations** (“holds”) that prevent oversell during checkout. The core insight is to treat *available inventory* as a **strictly consistent, transactionally protected counter**, while everything else (search, browsing, availability badges) can be **eventually consistent** and cached.

The elegant design keeps a single source of truth for stock mutations (reserve/commit/release) and makes those mutations **idempotent and stateful**. Instead of complex distributed locking, we use one boring invariant: *a reservation is only created if an atomic conditional update succeeds*—and every later step is just a safe state transition.

## What Makes This Hard

Naive implementations model “available stock” as a read-modify-write value (or a cache entry) and then bolt on “holds” later. Under concurrency, retries, and partial failures (payment succeeds but commit fails, client retries, workers crash), this creates two classic traps:

1. **Double-decrement / double-release**: the same user action (or retry) mutates stock multiple times.
2. **Ghost inventory**: expired holds don’t get released promptly, so you under-sell; or worse, holds get ignored and you oversell.
